# `namespaced_id_type!`

<SymbolMeta />

`namespaced_id_type!` defines a lightweight, copyable ID type for one category of stable namespaced identity. Upwell uses it for scopes and composition IDs; extension crates can use it when their public contract needs a distinct identifier category with the same literal grammar.

<SymbolSignature />

## Grammar

```text
namespaced_id_type!(
    <outer attributes>*
    <visibility> struct <type name>,
    <category literal>
)
```

`<outer attributes>` accepts normal Rust outer attributes, including documentation attributes and conditional-compilation attributes. `<visibility>` uses Rust's `vis` grammar, so `pub`, restricted visibility such as `pub(crate)`, or private visibility are accepted. `<type name>` is one Rust identifier. `<category literal>` is a Rust string literal used in validation errors; it is not a namespace restriction and does not need to follow the identifier syntax.

```rust
use upwell_core::namespaced_id_type;

namespaced_id_type!(
    /// Stable identity for a tenant extension's policy rule.
    #[derive(Default)]
    pub struct PolicyRuleId,
    "policy rule"
);
```

The macro itself adds `Clone`, `Copy`, `Debug`, `Eq`, `Hash`, `Ord`, `PartialEq`, and `PartialOrd`. Add only derives that do not conflict with those generated implementations.

## Generated API

For `PolicyRuleId`, the expansion creates a struct with private representation and these public inherent methods:

| Method | Result |
| --- | --- |
| `const fn new(&'static str) -> Result<Self, InvalidNamespacedId>` | validates and constructs the value |
| `const fn as_str(&self) -> &'static str` | complete canonical text |
| `const fn namespace(&self) -> &'static str` | text before the first slash |
| `const fn name(&self) -> &'static str` | alias for `local_path` |
| `const fn local_path(&self) -> &'static str` | non-empty text after the first slash |
| `const fn parts(&self) -> (&'static str, &'static str)` | namespace and local path |

It also implements `Display`, `AsRef<str>`, and `NamespacedIdType`. The trait provides runtime `is_in_namespace` and `shares_namespace_with` checks across different generated ID categories. Prefer the inherent const accessors when a value must be inspected in const code.

```rust
let id = PolicyRuleId::new("acme/retention").expect("static ID is valid");

assert_eq!(id.namespace(), "acme");
assert_eq!(id.local_path(), "retention");
assert_eq!(id.to_string(), "acme/retention");
```

## Literal validation and errors

`new` accepts only `&'static str`, because the generated value borrows its canonical text. The value must have at least two non-empty slash-separated segments. Every segment starts with a lowercase ASCII letter or digit; following bytes may additionally be `.`, `_`, or `-`. Nested local paths are valid.

Failures return `InvalidNamespacedId`, which records the category literal, rejected value, and an `IdErrorKind`:

| Error kind | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `MissingNamespace` | no slash, a leading/trailing slash, or an empty segment |
| `InvalidSegmentStart` | a segment starts with uppercase ASCII, punctuation, whitespace, or another unsupported byte |
| `InvalidCharacter` | a later byte is outside lowercase ASCII letters, digits, `.`, `_`, and `-` |

`InvalidNamespacedId` exposes `category`, `value`, `kind`, and `reason`; `reason` is an alias for `kind`. Its display text includes the category, literal, and readable validation reason.

## Pairing with `namespaced_id!`

Use `new` when a validation error should be handled or reported. Use [`namespaced_id!`](./namespaced_id) for a fixed literal that is part of source code and should fail compilation if malformed.

```rust
use upwell_core::{namespaced_id, namespaced_id_type};

namespaced_id_type!(pub struct MetricId, "metric");

const REQUESTS: MetricId = namespaced_id!(MetricId, "acme/requests");
```

## Extension authors

Define a new type only when identifiers must not be interchangeable with existing Upwell categories. Prefer existing public IDs such as `PluginId`, `ProtocolId`, `PluginSlotId`, `ContributionId`, and `ScopeId` when the value participates in those contracts.

Choose a category string that explains the value in diagnostics, preserve the type's literal IDs as compatibility surface, and keep the representation private. Do not serialize or reconstruct the generated fields directly; retain the canonical string and validate it through `new` at an input boundary.
